14. Coulomb Wave Functions
Mathematical Properties
14.1. Differential Equation, Series Expansions
Differential Equation
14.1.1  \frac{d^{2}w}{d\rho^{2}}+[1-\tfrac{2\eta}{\rho}\frac{L(L+1)}{\rho^{2}}]w=0 (\rho>0, \;-\infty\times\eta<\infty, L a non-negative integer)
The Coulomb wave equation has a regular singularity at \rho=0 with indices L+1 and -L; it has an irregular singularity at \rho=\infty.
General Solution
14.1.2   w=C_{1}F_{L}(\eta, \rho)+C_{2}G_{L}(\eta, \rho) (C_{1}, C_{2} constants) where F_{L}(\eta, \rho) is the regular Coulomb wave function and G_{L}(\eta, \rho) is the irregular (logarithmic) Coulomb wave function.
Regular Coulomb Wave Function F_{L}(\eta, \rho)
14.1.3   F_{L}(\eta, \rho)=C_{L}(\eta)\rho^{L+1}e^{-i\rho}M(L+1-i\eta,2L+2,2i\rho)
14.1.4  =C_{L}(\eta)\rho^{L+1}\Phi_{L}(\eta, \rho)
14.1.5  \Phi_{L}(\eta, \rho)=\sum\limits_{k=L+1}^{\infty}A_{k}^{L}(\eta)\rho^{k-L-1}
14.1.6   A_{L+1}^{L}=1,  A_{L+2}^{L}=\tfrac{\eta}{L+1}, (k+L)\langle k-L-1\rangle A_{k}^{L}=2\eta A_{k-1}^{L}-A_{k-2}^{L} (k>L+2)
14.1.7   C_{L}(\eta)=\tfrac{2^{L}e^{\tfrac{-\pi\eta}{2}}|\Gamma(L+1+i\eta)|}{\Gamma(2L+2)} (See chapter 6.)
